              <a href="#hooking-up" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">7.</span><span>Hooking It to the House</span></a>
              <a href="#fuel" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">8.</span><span>Fuel and Run Time</span></a>
              <a href="#co-safety" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">9.</span><span>Carbon Monoxide Rules</span></a>
              <a href="#upkeep" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">10.</span><span>Monthly Upkeep</span></a>
              <a href="#outgrown" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">11.</span><span>Signs You Need Bigger</span></a>
              <a href="#mistakes" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">12.</span><span>Common Mistakes</span></a>
              <a href="#faq" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">13.</span><span>FAQ</span></a>
        </nav>
      </details>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A small generator is the right outage tool for far more homes than the big-box displays suggest. If your loads are a refrigerator, a freezer, lights, the internet and a gas furnace blower, 2,000 to 4,500 watts of quiet inverter power does the job on a fraction of the fuel a 9,000-watt machine burns.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Six Small Generators, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We ranked these on published specifications (running and starting watts, tank size and listed run time, rated noise, weight), carbon monoxide shutoff and emissions certifications, warranty terms, and the patterns that recur in owner feedback. We did not conduct our own test program on these units. The order reflects how well each serves a typical household that wants essentials, not the most watts.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="defining-small" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Defining a Small Generator for Home Backup
          </h2>

          <p>
            In practice, small means a generator one or two adults can move without a truck ramp, with running output from roughly 1,600 to about 4,000 watts. Most are inverter generators with an enclosed case, 120-volt outlets only, and a tank of one to three and a half gallons. Weight runs from under 40 pounds for the suitcase units to about 100 pounds for the largest wheeled models.
          </p>

          <p>
            That size class exists because most outage loads are modest. A modern refrigerator averages around 100 to 150 watts over a day, a gas furnace blower draws a few hundred watts, LED lighting and internet gear add a couple of hundred more. The things that blow up a load list, central air conditioning, electric water heaters, electric ranges and dryers, well pumps on 240 volts, are the things a small generator simply does not attempt.
          </p>

          <p>
            The advantages compound. Small generators burn far less fuel, which matters when stations are closed. They are quieter, which matters at night and with close neighbors. They are cheaper to buy and easier to store. And they are far easier to wheel out and start in the dark, which is when outages tend to happen.
          </p>

          <p>
            The question to ask is not whether a small generator can power your house; it cannot. The question is whether it can power the parts of your house you genuinely need during an outage. For a large share of homes with gas heat, the answer is yes. For homes on a well, homes with electric heat, and homes in hot climates where air conditioning is a safety issue, our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">full outage generator rankings</a> cover the larger machines.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-it-powers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What a Small Generator Will Actually Power
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every motor needs a short burst of extra power to start, so a small generator's starting rating matters as much as its running rating. A refrigerator compressor that runs on 150 to 200 watts may need 1,000 to 2,000 watts for a second as it starts. The trick with a small unit is to let one motor start at a time rather than having the fridge, freezer and sump pump all kick on together.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
