          </div>

          <p>
            Read your appliance data plates. Running amps times 120 gives you a decent estimate of running watts. A plug-in watt meter on the refrigerator for a day is even better; it tells you both the running draw and the daily average, which is what matters for fuel planning. Older refrigerators and chest freezers can use two or three times what a new one does.
          </p>

          <p>
            Microwaves, coffee makers, toasters and hair dryers are the hidden budget-busters. Each draws 1,000 to 1,500 watts while running. On a 2,200-watt generator, run them one at a time with nothing else heavy on, and plan your meals around it. A propane camp stove outdoors is a better outage cooking tool than any electric appliance on a small generator.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
